 # HubSpot free-account probe
 
 A one-shot script that answers one question: does a free HubSpot account accept the API writes the desk needs
-(set the owner and the lead status on a contact, then delete it)? Result: the only real-service try, run by the author on one free HubSpot account, logs not shipped. In the first run create, lead-status write, read-back and permanent delete passed, and the owner list was refused (403 `MISSING_SCOPES`) until the token also had the owners read scope; after that a second run passed every step, and a third run, after a second user was added, listed two owners and passed again. Each run used 6 or 7 of the 8 calls and left no contact behind (the author's count from the script's own printout; no log is shipped). The tests in `tests/test_hubspot_probe.py` check the budget of 8 calls and the masking of the token against a local stand-in, not against HubSpot.
+(set the owner and the lead status on a contact, then delete it)? Result: the only real-service try, run by the author on one free HubSpot account, logs not shipped. In the first run create, lead-status write, read-back and permanent delete passed, and the owner list was refused (403 `MISSING_SCOPES`) until the token also had the owners read scope; after that a second run passed every step, and a third run, after a second user was added, listed two owners and passed again. Each run used 6 or 7 of the 8 calls and left no contact behind (the author's count from the script's own printout; no log is shipped). The tests in `tests/test_hubspot_probe.py` check that a run stays inside the call budget (`CALL_BUDGET` in the script is 8; the tests read that constant and do not pin the number itself) and the masking of the token, against a local stand-in, not against HubSpot.
 
 Run it on the free demo account only, never on an account that holds real contacts. Creating or assigning a contact can
 trigger things on the account side that the script cannot see: an owner assignment might send that user a notification
